{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 0, "model_name": "qwen/qwen3.5-122b-a10b", "content": "Raymond III was a powerful noble who ruled the County of Tripoli during the twelfth century. He became the count after his father died in battle against the Muslims. His early life involved significant political struggles within the Crusader states. He faced challenges from rival factions and external threats from Saladin. His leadership style was often cautious yet strategic. He served as regent for the young King Baldwin IV of Jerusalem. This role placed him at the center of Crusader politics. He had to manage relations with other barons and foreign powers. The kingdom faced internal divisions that threatened its stability. Raymond tried to maintain peace while defending the realm from invasion. His relationship with King Amalric was complex and sometimes strained. He later supported Baldwin IV against the ambitions of Guy of Lusignan. The Baldwin IV era saw many battles and diplomatic maneuvers. Raymond played a key role in these critical moments. He understood the military threats facing the Latin East. His experience helped shape the defense strategies of the kingdom.\n\n*Early Life and Inheritance*\nRaymond inherited his title at a young age. His father Raymond II was assassinated by the Assassins. This event left the young count vulnerable to enemies. He spent years consolidating his power in Tripoli. He faced opposition from local nobles who sought to weaken his rule. His marriage to Eschiva of Bures brought him additional lands. This union strengthened his position within the Kingdom of Jerusalem. He became one of the most influential figures in the region. His lands were crucial for trade and military movement. He managed to keep Tripoli independent for many years.\n\n*Regency and Political Struggles*\nRaymond served as regent for the leper king Baldwin IV. This position gave him immense authority over the kingdom. He navigated the treacherous waters of court politics. He opposed the激进 policies of some other barons. His caution often put him at odds with more aggressive leaders. He sought alliances with Byzantine forces to strengthen defenses. The internal conflict between factions grew worse over time. Raymond tried to mediate disputes but often failed. His regency ended when Baldwin IV reached adulthood. The king then took full control of the throne.\n\n*The Battle of Hattin and Aftermath*\nRaymond played a controversial role before the Battle of Hattin. He advised against attacking Saladin directly near the Horns of Hattin. His advice was ignored by Guy of Lusignan. The Crusader army suffered a catastrophic defeat. Raymond escaped the battle and returned to Tripoli. He refused to submit to Saladin after the fall of Jerusalem. He negotiated a truce to save his own county. His actions were criticized by some as cowardly. Others viewed his pragmatism as necessary for survival. He died in 1187 shortly after these events. His death marked the end of an era for the County of Tripoli.", "logprobs": [{"token": "Ray", "logprob": -0.0022097188}, {"token": "mond", "logprob": -1.549722e-06}, {"token": " III", "logprob": -0.003953633}, {"token": " was", "logprob": -0.116659276}, {"token": " a", "logprob": -0.010217293}, {"token": " powerful", "logprob": -0.73232806}, {"token": " noble", "logprob": -0.0122804595}, {"token": " who", "logprob": -1.2689976}, {"token": " ruled", "logprob": -0.06893431}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.029771687}, {"token": " County", "logprob": -0.00083457475}, {"token": " of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " Trip", "logprob": -4.768373e-07}, {"token": "oli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": " during", "logprob": -0.013350397}, {"token": " the", "logprob": -0.046934616}, {"token": " tw", "logprob": -0.013859203}, {"token": "elfth", "logprob": -1.4186006e-05}, {"token": " century", "logprob": -6.198902e-06}, {"token": ".", "logprob": -0.059866212}, {"token": " He", "logprob": -0.18288508}, {"token"